---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Invitation from '../components/Invitation.astro';
import Process from '../components/Process.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Fractional AI support & delivery"
  description="Experienced AI support alongside your CTO or internal advocate. Assess opportunities, make the case to leadership, build agents and develop ownership in your team."
  service="Fractional AI consultancy and delivery"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap interior-hero">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow red">Fractional AI support & delivery</p><h1>
          Move AI forward.<br />With experienced<br />help alongside you.
        </h1><p class="lead">
          You have opportunities to explore and a business to keep running. Get ongoing help
          deciding what is worth doing, making the case, and getting it built.
        </p><a class="button" href="/enquire/?need=business"
          >Discuss fractional support <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
        ><p class="availability">Contract and fractional engagements. Remote preferred.</p>
      </div><div class="small-paper" aria-hidden="true">
        <span>Worth doing?</span><span>Let’s work<br />that out.</span><b>↗</b>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="wrap service-summary">
    <span>A business goal to work towards.</span><span>Help from the case to the build.</span><span
      >Ownership inside your team.</span
    >
  </div>
  <div class="wrap reading-layout section">
    <nav class="contents" aria-label="On this page">
      <p>On this page</p><a href="#making-the-case">Making the case</a><a href="#what-we-can-do"
        >What we can work on</a
      ><a href="#recent-experience">Recent experience</a><a href="#questions">Practical questions</a
      ><a class="text-link" href="/enquire/?need=business">Tell me what’s on your mind ↗</a>
    </nav>
    <div class="prose">
      <section id="making-the-case">
        <h2>When you need more than enthusiasm.</h2><p class="lead">
          You can see that AI could be useful. Turning that into a proposal people can assess is
          another piece of work.
        </p><p>
          You may already have a CTO or someone pushing AI forward. They need time, technical
          backing, and a case leadership can consider. I work alongside that person, helping them
          explore the opportunities, test assumptions and explain what is worth pursuing.
        </p><p>
          We start with a business goal. That might mean exploring a new customer channel, improving
          access to business knowledge, or creating a workflow nobody currently has capacity to run.
          We look at the value, the effort and the uncertainties before deciding what to build.
        </p><blockquote>What useful work isn’t happening today?</blockquote><p>
          That question often opens up better possibilities. An agent might research more potential
          customers than your team could reasonably investigate, or keep a business tool up to date
          using context already sitting in documents and emails. The aim is to add something
          worthwhile, with the people affected involved from the start.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section id="what-we-can-do">
        <h2>What we can work on.</h2><p>
          Your needs determine the mix of advice, engineering and team support. An engagement can
          develop across several of these areas.
        </p><h3>Opportunity and business case</h3><p>
          Work with leadership and internal advocates to identify useful applications, assess
          feasibility, and propose a specific next step. Small experiments can make the trade-offs
          clearer before a larger commitment.
        </p><h3>Agents, workflows and integrations</h3><p>
          Build internal or customer-facing agents, connect existing tools, and make shared context
          available where people need it. That can include MCP servers, skill libraries, CRM
          extensions, dashboards and visible automations. Sometimes an existing product is the
          sensible choice; sometimes a small custom build is needed.
        </p><h3>Team adoption and internal ownership</h3><p>
          Help people work confidently with the tools they will use. Some are comfortable directing
          an agent; others need a familiar interface and a workflow they can see. We account for
          that in the implementation.
        </p><p>
          I can also support an in-house specialist taking responsibility for the agent
          infrastructure: the shared skills, permissions, context and conventions that need ongoing
          care. The aim is for your team to have the knowledge and ownership to keep developing the
          work.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
  <Process />
  <div class="wrap reading-layout section" id="recent-experience">
    <aside class="contents">
      <p>Experience, in context</p><p class="aside-note">
        Two examples of how the work can develop. Client names are omitted here.
      </p>
    </aside><div class="prose">
      <h2>Recent experience.</h2><article id="travel">
        <p class="eyebrow red">Travel</p><h3>Test a channel. Follow the evidence.</h3><p>
          A travel business was exploring a possible AI customer-discovery channel. The work started
          with a prototype to investigate that opportunity.
        </p><p>
          The findings helped redirect investment towards a customer-facing agent on the company’s
          own website. The development team was supported and trained to take ownership of the
          implementation.
        </p><p>
          The useful outcome was a better-informed direction and work the team could take forward.
        </p>
      </article><article id="ev-mapping">
        <p class="eyebrow red">EV mapping</p><h3>Back the people carrying it forward.</h3><p>
          Work with an EV mapping company included AI experiments across management teams and
          training for an in-house AI specialist.
        </p><p>
          Alongside exploring applications, the work supported someone inside the business to
          develop the knowledge and confidence to carry it forward. That combination of practical
          exploration and internal ownership informs how I approach fractional support.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
  <section class="faq-section section" id="questions">
    <div class="wrap reading-layout">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The practical bits</p><h2>A few things<br />you might ask.</h2>
      </div><div class="faqs">
        <details open>
          <summary>Can you work alongside our CTO?</summary><p>
            Yes. Support can sit alongside your existing technical leadership. We agree who makes
            decisions, who does the work, and who owns it internally.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can we start with a defined project?</summary><p>
            Yes. A specific opportunity can be a starting point. We can also discuss ongoing support
            if you need help across several decisions or pieces of work.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How are scope and fees agreed?</summary><p>
            We discuss the outcome, the work involved, and the level of ongoing support before
            agreeing an engagement. You can send an initial brief even if the scope is still taking
            shape.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do you work remotely?</summary><p>
            Yes. Remote engagements are preferred. In-person work in Bristol can be arranged around
            the calendar.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Are you available for permanent roles?</summary><p>
            I take on fractional and contract work. If you are a recruiter with an opportunity that
            fits, send the brief and the expected working arrangement.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Invitation title="What are you trying to move forward?" />
</Layout>
